WEBVTT

1
00:00:06.196 --> 00:00:06.936
are everywhere.

2
00:00:07.397 --> 00:00:09.858
We use them every day but hardly think about them.

3
00:00:10.439 --> 00:00:14.942
They are so much a part of daily life that the English language is full of phrases about

4
00:00:15.022 --> 00:00:20.405
roads. When we say that a project "hits a bump in the road," we mean a problem has

5
00:00:20.465 --> 00:00:26.009
occurred, and a person who takes "the road less traveled" has chosen an unusual way of life.

6
00:00:27.490 --> 00:00:32.353
Roads developed along with human civilization, so we have to go a long way back to find a

7
00:00:32.413 --> 00:00:33.393
time before roads.

8
00:00:33.893 --> 00:00:39.935
During the Ice Age seventy thousand years ago, humans lived in caves and hunted wild animals with weapons

9
00:00:39.975 --> 00:00:41.255
made of sticks and stones.

10
00:00:41.835 --> 00:00:46.677
The ground was covered with snow, and hunters easily lost their way in the thick forests.

11
00:00:47.257 --> 00:00:50.918
They would mark their tracks with branches so that they could find their way home.

12
00:00:52.298 --> 00:00:54.319
That is how the first paths were created.

13
00:00:55.099 --> 00:00:58.480
Around 10,000 BC, the earth's climate began to warm up.

14
00:00:59.104 --> 00:01:03.767
Tribes of people started to live in villages, where they grew crops and kept tame animals.

15
00:01:04.327 --> 00:01:07.348
They also learned how to make tools out of bronze and iron.

16
00:01:08.009 --> 00:01:13.592
They needed roads to travel to each other's villages, so they cleared paths through forests and across plains

17
00:01:13.972 --> 00:01:15.973
and built simple dirt roads with their tools.

18
00:01:17.474 --> 00:01:21.076
They used primitive carts to carry heavy loads on these first roads.

19
00:01:22.657 --> 00:01:25.738
Dirt roads, however, twisted and turned and did not last long.

20
00:01:26.439 --> 00:01:28.880
Also they were not convenient in bad weather.

21
00:01:29.524 --> 00:01:34.447
Rain would turn the dirt to mud, making it impossible for people or animals to pull their carts.

22
00:01:35.708 --> 00:01:38.910
Roads could remain unusable for days after a heavy rain.

23
00:01:39.590 --> 00:01:45.094
Over time the footsteps and wheels from all the traffic would create deep grooves in the roads.

24
00:01:45.714 --> 00:01:49.236
These narrow ruts made walking and pulling carts even more difficult.

25
00:01:50.197 --> 00:01:55.080
But despite all these problems, it was a long time before anyone improved on dirt roads.

26
00:01:56.416 --> 00:02:01.301
The first modern roads were built at the height of the Roman Empire in the first century AD.

27
00:02:01.701 --> 00:02:03.643
The Roman Empire was very powerful.

28
00:02:04.144 --> 00:02:07.747
It ruled over three continents and had a very large army.

29
00:02:08.428 --> 00:02:13.453
In order to move their soldiers around the vast empire, the Roman emperors needed well-built roads.

30
00:02:14.274 --> 00:02:16.496
Fortunately the Romans were great engineers.

31
00:02:17.057 --> 00:02:20.200
The roads they built ran perfectly straight for hundreds of kilometers.

32
00:02:21.484 --> 00:02:26.207
The Romans built their roads from stones instead of dirt, and laid gravel and cement over the stones

33
00:02:26.447 --> 00:02:28.508
to give the roads a hard, smooth surface.

34
00:02:29.108 --> 00:02:33.571
They even carefully shaped their roads so that the center was a little higher than the edges.

35
00:02:34.391 --> 00:02:38.353
That way, rainwater would not collect in pools but would run off to the sides.

36
00:02:39.034 --> 00:02:42.576
Roman roads were the first to have a drainage system to keep them from flooding.

37
00:02:43.916 --> 00:02:48.879
Another famous saying is that "all roads lead to Rome," and during the time of the Roman Empire,

38
00:02:49.239 --> 00:02:50.440
this was certainly true.

39
00:02:50.988 --> 00:02:56.273
But it remained true for long after and, in some ways, it is still true today.

40
00:02:56.893 --> 00:03:01.177
The Roman roads were so convenient that everyone used them, not just the soldiers.

41
00:03:02.158 --> 00:03:06.922
People traveled from town to town, traded goods, and sent messages using the Roman roads.

42
00:03:08.263 --> 00:03:13.687
Villages and towns located along the roads grew larger, while towns farther away became smaller.

43
00:03:14.368 --> 00:03:19.012
The Romans built their roads so well that many of them lasted until the nineteenth century!

44
00:03:19.688 --> 00:03:25.571
More than a thousand years after the last Roman emperor died, people were still using the straight, durable

45
00:03:25.611 --> 00:03:28.053
roads that once connected the Roman Empire.

46
00:03:28.613 --> 00:03:32.475
In fact, many of Europe's modern highways follow those same routes today.

47
00:03:34.016 --> 00:03:39.599
By the time of the Industrial Revolution in the late eighteenth century, new road technologies were needed.

48
00:03:40.319 --> 00:03:46.883
Goods manufactured in factories had to be transported to and from great, crowded cities like London and Paris.

49
00:03:47.643 --> 00:03:51.465
The train was invented, and people began to travel much more than before.

50
00:03:52.165 --> 00:03:57.066
An engineer from Scotland named John McAdam revolutionized the way roads were built.

51
00:03:58.467 --> 00:04:01.547
His method, called the macadam, is still used today.

52
00:04:02.188 --> 00:04:08.729
He crushed stone into three specific sizes and built his roads in layers, using the largest stones for

53
00:04:08.769 --> 00:04:11.370
the bottom layer and the smallest stones for the top layer.

54
00:04:12.110 --> 00:04:17.392
By wetting the road and then compacting it with a heavy roller, the stones locked together to create

55
00:04:17.452 --> 00:04:18.332
a firm surface.

56
00:04:19.540 --> 00:04:25.463
Macadam roads were more durable, less expensive, and easier to build, but they were very dusty.

57
00:04:26.484 --> 00:04:30.526
With the invention of the automobile, a smoother, cleaner surface was needed.

58
00:04:31.047 --> 00:04:33.248
Today paved roads are covered in asphalt.

59
00:04:33.888 --> 00:04:39.711
Asphalt was first used in France in 1824 on the Champs-Elysees boulevard in Paris.

60
00:04:40.312 --> 00:04:45.855
Asphalt was not popular at first because it was rough, but a few years later, a man called

61
00:04:45.975 --> 00:04:47.676
Edward de Smedt perfected it.

62
00:04:49.184 --> 00:04:55.406
In 1872 he produced a higher-grade asphalt, and the first street to be paved using his asphalt was

63
00:04:55.446 --> 00:04:56.946
Fifth Avenue in New York.

64
00:04:57.507 --> 00:05:01.968
By the turn of the twentieth century, cars, bicycles, and motorbikes were everywhere.

65
00:05:02.628 --> 00:05:08.690
After World War II cities were getting overcrowded, and people began to experience the first traffic jams.

66
00:05:09.330 --> 00:05:11.791
The first highways were built in the 1960s.

67
00:05:12.931 --> 00:05:15.452
Highways are designed for safe, fast travel.

68
00:05:16.736 --> 00:05:19.939
They have no traffic lights, no stop signs, and no parking.

69
00:05:20.479 --> 00:05:23.502
Today highways are the most popular method of ground travel.

70
00:05:24.103 --> 00:05:30.388
Even though advances in road-building have greatly improved travel, many cities are now polluted and overcrowded with traffic.

71
00:05:30.909 --> 00:05:35.312
And traffic jams are not just bad for drivers, they are also bad for the environment.

72
00:05:35.913 --> 00:05:39.756
Cars and trucks contribute to unhealthy smog and global warming.

73
00:05:40.657 --> 00:05:45.922
While new electric and hybrid cars are much less polluting, many cities have decided to make parts of

74
00:05:45.962 --> 00:05:48.023
their downtown off limits to cars.

75
00:05:48.364 --> 00:05:53.428
The historic downtown of Copenhagen in Denmark is Europe's oldest and largest car-free zone.

76
00:05:53.988 --> 00:05:58.492
People walk its streets just like people did on the first roads thousands of years ago.

77
00:05:59.993 --> 00:06:04.557
Roads have been part of human civilization since hunters first marked their footsteps in the snow.

78
00:06:05.137 --> 00:06:10.762
So whether we travel by foot, by car, or in some new vehicle of the future, wherever we

79
00:06:10.802 --> 00:06:13.024
go, there will always be roads.
